.TH COMPARTMENT-ROOT 8 "June 2026" "compartment 1.3.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
compartment-root \- full-namespace process isolation (requires root)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compartment-root
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compartment-root
creates a fully isolated container using Linux namespaces, then drops
privileges and executes the given command. Requires root. Zero dependencies.
.PP
Isolation is applied in order:
.IP 1. 4
.B clone()
with new UTS, mount, PID, IPC, net, user, and cgroup namespaces.
.IP 2.
.B pivot_root
\- new root filesystem, bind-mounted
.B nosuid,nodev
onto itself first (stronger than chroot).
.IP 3.
.IR /proc " (fresh procfs), " /sys " (read-only sysfs), " /dev
(tmpfs with device nodes bind-mounted from the old root), masked
.IR /proc " paths, isolated hostname."
All of this happens
.I before
the old root is detached \- see
.B HARDENING DETAILS
below.
.IP 4.
.B Capability drop
\- all capabilities removed from bounding set except those named via
.BR \-\-cap\-allowed .
Uses raw prctl + capset (no libcap dependency).
.IP 5.
.B Privilege drop
\- setgid/setuid to service user.
.IP 6.
.B Environment sanitization
and
.B audit logging
(same as
.BR compartment-user (1)).
.IP 7.
.B FD cleanup
\- close all file descriptors >= 3 before the seccomp filter is installed
.RI ( close_range
or fallback loop).
.IP 8.
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS " (unconditional), " PR_SET_PDEATHSIG ,
then a fork: the command runs as a child of a minimal
.B PID 1
reaper inside the pid namespace.
.IP 9.
.B seccomp BPF
\- raw BPF filter, no libseccomp. A built-in deny-list is installed unless
.BR \-\-block ", " \-\-seccomp\-allowed
or a profile supplies one.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Profile
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-profile " NAME|FILE"
Load policy from a profile file.
Only
.I /etc/compartment/NAME.conf
is searched;
.I $HOME
is never consulted.
An explicit file path (containing \fB/\fR) loads directly.
The file and its containing directory must be owned by root and must not be
group- or world-writable.
Both the
.BI \-\-profile= FILE
and
.BI \-\-profile " FILE"
forms are accepted.
.SS Namespace
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-rootdir " DIR"
Root filesystem directory for pivot_root. Required.
Must be absolute and contain no \fB..\fR components.
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-uid " UID"
Override UID for privilege drop inside the container.
.TP
.BI \-g ", " \-\-gid " GID"
Override GID for privilege drop.
.TP
.BI \-U ", " \-\-username " USER"
Service user for privilege drop. Required.
Looked up via
.BR getpwnam (3)
in the
.B host
.IR /etc/passwd ,
before
.BR clone (2)
\- not in the container's. For a user that exists only inside the
container, give
.BR \-\-uid " and " \-\-gid
explicitly; the name is then used only for diagnostics.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-netns " NAME"
Join an existing network namespace
.RI ( /var/run/netns/NAME ).
Name must not contain \fB/\fR.
.B Known limitation:
the container always gets a new user namespace, and
.BR setns (2)
requires
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
in the user namespace that
.I owns
the target network namespace. A namespace created on the host (by
.BR "ip netns add" ,
say) is owned by the initial user namespace, so joining it fails with
.BR EPERM .
Use
.B \-\-loopback
in the container's own network namespace instead, or place the container
inside an already-joined namespace from outside.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loopback
Bring up loopback interface in the new network namespace.
.TP
.BI \-C ", " \-\-cgroup " PATH"
Assign the container process to a cgroup. Repeatable.
Path must be absolute, contain no \fB..\fR components, and resolve
(after
.BR realpath (3))
to a location under \fI/sys/fs/cgroup/\fR. The
.I cgroup.procs
file must already exist; it is never created.
.TP
.BI \-M ", " \-\-mount\-mask " PATH"
Hide \fIPATH\fR inside the container: an empty read-only tmpfs over a
directory, a bind of \fI/dev/null\fR over anything else. Repeatable.
Path must be absolute and contain no \fB..\fR components. A path that
does not exist on the running kernel is skipped; a path that exists and
cannot be masked is a fatal error.
.SS Capabilities
.TP
.BI \-A ", " \-\-cap\-allowed " CAP"
Preserve a capability for the service user. Repeatable.
Accepts \fBCAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\fR, \fBnet_bind_service\fR, or a
raw number (0\-63). Uses
.BR PR_SET_KEEPCAPS ,
raw
.BR capset (2),
and
.BR PR_CAP_AMBIENT_RAISE .
.SS "Syscalls (seccomp BPF)"
.TP
.BI \-a ", " \-\-seccomp\-allowed " SYSCALL"
Allow-list mode: only permit this syscall. Repeatable.
All other syscalls return EPERM.
.TP
.BI \-B ", " \-\-block " SYSCALL"
Deny-list mode: block this syscall. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-seccomp
Disable seccomp entirely.
.PP
With none of the above and no \fBblock\fR/\fBallow\fR lines in the
profile, a built-in deny-list is installed (43 syscalls on x86\-64):
ptrace and process_vm_readv/writev; mount, umount2, pivot_root, chroot,
unshare, setns and the new mount API (open_tree, move_mount, fsopen,
fsmount, fsconfig, fspick, mount_setattr); open_by_handle_at and
name_to_handle_at; reboot, kexec_load, kexec_file_load, init_module,
finit_module, delete_module; keyctl, add_key, request_key; bpf,
userfaultfd, perf_event_open, io_uring_setup/enter/register; acct, swapon,
swapoff, settimeofday, clock_settime, clock_adjtime, adjtimex;
pidfd_getfd; ioperm and iopl. Asking for seccomp with an empty filter is
refused rather than run unfiltered.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BI \-E ", " \-\-env\-deny " VAR"
Strip environment variable. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-env\-allow " VAR"
Allow-list mode: keep only this variable. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-sanitize
Do not strip environment variables.
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be applied without executing.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print actions to stderr. Combined with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR it also lists
the built-in parts of the policy: the mount plan, every masked path, the
device nodes, the init reaper and the blocked syscalls by name.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Log events to stderr and to a file.
.TP
.BI \-L ", " \-\-audit\-log " DIR"
Audit log \fBdirectory\fR (implies \fB\-\-audit\fR). The log file
inside it is named \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.log\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check system support and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage summary.
.SH HARDENING DETAILS
.SS Mount order and mount_too_revealing
Mounting a fresh
.IR procfs " or " sysfs
from inside a user namespace is gated by the kernel's
.BR mount_too_revealing ()
check: it succeeds only while a fully visible mount of the same filesystem
already exists in the current mount namespace. The container therefore
does
.BR pivot_root (2)
first, mounts
.IR /proc " and " /sys ,
populates
.IR /dev ,
applies the masks, and detaches the old root
.RB ( "umount2(MNT_DETACH)" )
last. Detaching it earlier makes the
.I /proc
mount fail with
.BR EPERM .
.SS Container root
.I rootdir
is bind-mounted onto itself
.RB ( MS_BIND | MS_REC )
and then remounted
.BR nosuid,nodev .
The remount uses
.BR mount_setattr (2)
with
.B AT_RECURSIVE
where available (Linux 5.12+) so submounts are covered too, and falls back
to
.BR MS_REMOUNT | MS_BIND ,
which changes the per-mount flags only \- the superblock and the host's
view of the same filesystem are untouched. A setuid-root binary inside
.I rootdir
therefore does not elevate.
.SS /dev
A
.B tmpfs
is mounted on
.I /dev
.RB ( nosuid,noexec ;
deliberately not
.BR nodev ),
and
.IR null ", " zero ", " full ", " random ", " urandom " and " tty
are bind-mounted into it from the old root.
.BR mknod (2)
cannot be used: the kernel checks
.B CAP_MKNOD
against the initial user namespace, so it always fails here. Each bind is
fatal on failure.
.IR /dev/pts " is created and " /dev/fd ", " /dev/stdin ", " /dev/stdout
and
.I /dev/stderr
are symlinked into
.IR /proc/self/fd .
.SS /proc and /sys masking
.I /proc
is a fresh procfs
.RB ( nosuid,noexec,nodev ).
.I /sys
is a fresh read-only sysfs
.RB ( ro,nosuid,noexec,nodev )
with
.I /sys/firmware
masked; if the kernel refuses the sysfs mount,
.I /sys
is covered with an empty read-only tmpfs instead.
.PP
Masked by default inside the container \- an empty read-only tmpfs over a
directory, a bind of
.I /dev/null
over a file:
.IR /proc/acpi ", " /proc/bus ", " /proc/fs ", " /proc/irq ", "
.IR /proc/kallsyms ", " /proc/kcore ", " /proc/keys ", "
.IR /proc/latency_stats ", " /proc/modules ", " /proc/sched_debug ", "
.IR /proc/scsi ", " /proc/sys ", " /proc/sysrq-trigger ", "
.IR /proc/timer_list ", " /proc/timer_stats .
Entries absent on the running kernel are skipped; an entry that exists and
cannot be masked aborts the run. Additional paths can be masked with
.BR \-\-mount\-mask .
.SS User namespace
The parent writes
.B deny
to
.I /proc/<pid>/setgroups
before the gid map (skipped when the policy keeps
.BR CAP_SETGID )
and clears its own supplementary groups before
.BR clone (2).
The map itself defaults to the identity map \fB0 0 65536\fR, which gives
a capability boundary but
.B no uid isolation:
container uid 0 is host uid 0 for DAC purposes. Use the
.BR uid-map " and " gid-map
profile directives to map onto a subuid range instead \-
.I rootdir
must then be owned by the mapped host uid.
.SS Container init
The command does not run as PID 1. PID 1 of the new pid namespace is a
minimal reaper that sets
.B PR_SET_PDEATHSIG
to
.BR SIGKILL ,
forwards
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ", " SIGHUP " and " SIGQUIT
to the command, reaps orphans, and exits with the command's status
(128+\fIn\fR if it was killed by signal \fIn\fR). The reaper is inside
every namespace and carries the same dropped capabilities, dropped uid and
.B no-new-privs
as the command, but it is deliberately
.B not
covered by the seccomp filter \- the command installs that for itself
after the fork, so an allow-list policy that does not mention
.BR wait4 / kill / rt_sigaction
cannot break the reaper. It execs nothing. Without it a command
with no signal handlers would ignore Ctrl\-C, orphans would never be
reaped, and killing
.B compartment-root
would leave the container running.
.SS Privilege drop sequence
.IP 1. 4
Drop bounding-set capabilities (while still root, needs CAP_SETPCAP).
.IP 2.
.B setgid/setuid
to service user.
.IP 3.
If
.B \-\-cap\-allowed
is used: restore named capabilities via capset + ambient raise.
.IP 4.
.B PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0)
\- prevent ptrace from outside.
.IP 5.
Environment sanitization.
.IP 6.
Close all FDs >= 3.
.IP 7.
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS " and " PR_SET_PDEATHSIG ,
then fork under the PID 1 reaper.
.IP 8.
.B seccomp BPF
filter applied.
.IP 9.
.B execvp
the command.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic container:
.B compartment-root \-c /srv/jail \-U svc \-\- /bin/sh
.TP
With capabilities and audit:
.B compartment-root \-c /srv/jail \-U svc \-l \-\-audit \-A net_bind_service \-\- /usr/bin/myapp
.TP
With profile:
.B compartment-root \-\-profile container \-\- /bin/sh
.TP
Preview:
.B compartment-root \-\-profile container \-\-dry\-run \-\- /bin/sh
.SH EXIT STATUS
The container command's exit status is passed through, so any value in
0\-255 is possible.
.TP
.B 0
Command executed successfully inside the container.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration error, namespace creation failure, or privilege drop failure
\- or the command itself exiting 1.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
The command was killed by signal \fIn\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/compartment/*.conf
System-wide profile files.
compartment-root reads no other location.
.TP
.I /var/log/compartment/YYYY\-MM\-DD.log
Default audit log, when
.B \-\-audit
is given without
.BR \-\-audit\-log :
one file per day in a directory created mode 0700.
It must be owned by root and mode 0700; compartment-root refuses to run
if auditing was requested and the directory cannot be used safely.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux >= 4.6 (CLONE_NEWCGROUP). Requires root or equivalent capabilities.
No external libraries (no libseccomp, no libcap).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR compartment-user (1),
.BR namespaces (7),
.BR pivot_root (2),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR seccomp (2),
.BR cgroups (7)
.PP
